      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While C. perfringennes doesn't exhibit visible signs, its enzymatic toxin can cause damage to red blood cells, leading to hemolysis, which typically goes unnoticed until the affected tissue is digested and enters the bloodstream. However, symptoms typically develop within 9-16 hours after ingestion, allowing for early detection through foodborne illness reporting systems or patient complaints. Salad bar incidents or outbreaks often go undetected until consumer complaints surface, when cooking temperatures have been suboptimal or recent pathogen release occurred.",
